+++ b/docs/adr/adr-20261006-001-discount-allocation-by-vat-rate-group.md
@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+# ADR-001: Discount allocation by VAT-rate group with r2 per group
+
+- Status: accepted (decision del usuario; contabilidad no la ha revisado)
+- Change: discount-codes-invoice
+- Date: 2026-10-06
+
+## Context
+The spec requires VAT on the discounted base for orders mixing normal (21%) and reduced (10%) lines, leaves `lineas[]` pre-discount, and delegates allocation and rounding to design. The resulting global `iva` and `total` are public output and must be reproducible to the cent.
+
+## Decision
+Group taxable bases by rate (`baseN`, `baseR`, each `r2` of its line importes). `descuento = r2(subtotal * pct / 100)`; `descR = r2(baseR * pct / 100)`; `descN = r2(descuento - descR)` (normal group absorbs the rounding residual). `iva = r2(r2(r2(baseN - descN) * 0.21) + r2(r2(baseR - descR) * 0.10))`. The no-code path keeps today's per-line VAT sum untouched.
+
+## Alternatives
+- Per-line proportional allocation with largest-remainder cents: more code and per-line results that are never reported.
+- Single blended rate on the discounted subtotal: incorrect for mixed rates.
+- Reusing the code path with 0% for no-code orders: per-group VAT differs by cents from per-line VAT, breaking historical equality.
+
+## Consequences
+Easier: hand-verifiable formulas, allocation sums exactly to `descuento`, matches per-rate tax breakdown practice. Harder: with a code, `Σ lineas[].iva != iva`; two output paths must be kept in sync for shipping/total. Reversible (internal formula, no persisted data), but any change shifts `iva` cents on mixed orders, so accounting must confirm.
